---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Box from '../../components/arch/Box.astro';
import Edge from '../../components/arch/Edge.astro';
import Group from '../../components/arch/Group.astro';
import Label from '../../components/arch/Label.astro';
import Cell from '../../components/arch/Cell.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/arch/Legend.astro';

const stripKinds = ['buffer', 'buffer', 'plain', 'plain', 'plain', 'buffer', 'buffer'] as const;
const stripIds = ['K', 'Q', 'A', '7', 'J', '9', 'W'];
const stripMain = ['-200', '-100', '0', '100', '200', '300', '400'];
// Strip figure geometry: cells of 48 on a 56 pitch, starting at y = 56.
const sy = (i: number) => 56 + i * 56;

// Axis figure: one quadrant per orientation x direction.
type Quad = { x: number; y: number; label: string; note?: string; vertical: boolean; forward: boolean; line: string };
const quads: Quad[] = [
  { x: 16, y: 20, label: 'vertical / forward', note: 'the default', vertical: true, forward: true, line: "main 'y', polarity +1, feed 'start'" },
  { x: 364, y: 20, label: 'vertical / reverse', note: 'roll-up', vertical: true, forward: false, line: "main 'y', polarity -1, feed 'end'" },
  { x: 16, y: 290, label: 'horizontal / forward', vertical: false, forward: true, line: "main 'x', polarity +1, feed 'start'" },
  { x: 364, y: 290, label: 'horizontal / reverse', vertical: false, forward: false, line: "main 'x', polarity -1, feed 'end'" },
];
---
<Architecture
  title="Motion"
  subtitle="The strip, index to position, wrapping, the axis, one tick."
  eyebrow="Physics"
>
  <p>
    A reel is not a column of three symbols. It is a <strong>strip</strong> of
    <code>M = bufferStart + visibleCells + bufferEnd</code> symbols that
    marches along one screen axis and wraps around. Every position on screen
    is computed from a symbol's <em>array index</em> each frame, nothing is
    accumulated, and the only state the motion layer keeps is how far the strip
    has travelled since it was last snapped. This page covers that layer
    (<code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/ReelMotion.ts</code>), the axis it is
    projected through, the drive model, and what happens in one ticker frame.
  </p>

  <h2>The strip</h2>

  <Diagram height={500} caption="One reel's symbol array, with the main coordinate each index gets at pitch 100 and off 0. Index 0 is always the symbol nearest the start edge, whatever direction the reel travels; the dashed outline is the mask.">
    <Label x={70} y={40} text="i" anchor="end" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    <Label x={124} y={40} text="strip" anchor="middle" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    <Label x={470} y={40} text="main" anchor="end" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    {stripKinds.map((kind, i) => (
      <Cell x={100} y={sy(i)} size={48} label={stripIds[i]} kind={kind} />
    ))}
    {stripKinds.map((_, i) => (
      <Label x={70} y={sy(i) + 29} text={`${i}`} anchor="end" mono tone="fg" />
    ))}
    {stripMain.map((m, i) => (
      <Label x={470} y={sy(i) + 29} text={m} anchor="end" mono tone="fg" />
    ))}
    <rect x={94} y={sy(2) - 6} width={60} height={3 * 56 - 8 + 12} rx="8" class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 4 3" />
    <Label x={176} y={sy(0) + 29} text="bufferStart (2)" />
    <Label x={176} y={sy(1) + 29} text="feed edge, forward" tone="faint" />
    <Label x={176} y={sy(3) + 29} text="visible (3): the window" />
    <Label x={176} y={sy(5) + 29} text="bufferEnd (2)" />
    <Label x={176} y={sy(6) + 29} text="exit edge, forward" tone="faint" />
    <Edge from={[560, sy(0) + 8]} to={[560, sy(6) + 40]} kind="primary" label="travel" />
    <Label x={360} y={482} text="main = (i - bufferStart) * pitch + off" size={14} mono tone="fg" anchor="middle" />
  </Diagram>

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <Legend items={[
      { kind: 'muted', label: 'buffer cell', note: 'outside the mask, random-filled by slot kind' },
      { kind: 'subtle', label: 'visible cell', note: 'inside the window, what getVisibleSymbols() reports' },
      { kind: 'accent', label: 'Reel', note: 'owns the array and picks ids' },
      { kind: 'primary', label: 'ReelMotion', note: 'writes every main coordinate' },
    ]} />
  </div>

  <p>
    Read the figure as a table: <code>pitch = cellMain + mainGap</code>
    (<code>ReelMotion.slotPitch</code>), and with <code>bufferStart = 2</code>
    index 2 is the first visible cell at main 0, index 0 sits one buffer above
    the window at -200, and the window spans <code>0 .. 3 * pitch - gap</code>.
    <code>getCellMain(cell) = (cell - bufferStart) * pitch</code> is the same
    formula read back by visible cell number. <code>snapToGrid()</code> sets
    <code>travel = rot = off = 0</code> and re-renders, so after a snap every
    symbol sits exactly on its grid slot. Buffers are geometric:
    <code>bufferStart</code> is the smaller coordinate on every reel, forward
    or reverse (ADR 016 section 3.4).
  </p>

  <h2>Derive from index, never accumulate</h2>

  <p>
    <code>ReelMotion.advance(delta)</code> does not move symbols by
    <code>delta</code>. It adds <code>polarity * delta</code> to a single
    <code>_travel</code> number, derives the whole-wrap count
    <code>rot = floor(travel / pitch)</code>, rotates the array until the
    applied count matches, and then writes every symbol's position from scratch
    with the formula above. ADR 018 (<code>docs/adr/018-motion-contract.md</code>)
    is the reason: a property suite found that the old accumulate-and-compare
    model skipped a wrap at exact slot boundaries, which lands the frame one
    symbol short. Deriving makes the strip's laws true by construction and the
    suite in <code>packages/pixi-reels/tests/contract/</code> runs them in CI
    against all four orientation × direction combinations.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rigidity and order.</strong> <code>main(symbols[i+1]) - main(symbols[i]) === pitch</code> and the array is strictly ascending by main coordinate. Array index <em>is</em> screen order. <code>getSymbolAt</code>, <code>getVisibleSymbols</code>, <code>placeSymbols</code> and the z-index formula all index by array position because of this.</li>
    <li><strong>Any step size is legal.</strong> There is no "at most one wrap per call" precondition. A 3-pitch step applies three rotations in the same <code>advance</code>. The half-slot cap in <code>StandardMode</code> is now only for smoothness.</li>
    <li><strong>Inverse and additivity.</strong> <code>advance(d); advance(-d)</code> restores the exact state and <code>advance(a); advance(b)</code> equals <code>advance(a+b)</code>, so frame rate cannot change where a reel ends up.</li>
    <li><strong>Never write a main coordinate outside the motion layer.</strong> <code>Reel</code> reads <code>view[mainProp]</code> back in <code>beginMotion</code>, <code>notifyLanded</code> and <code>_replaceSymbol</code> to recover which slot a view is in. The one documented exception is <code>ReelSymbol.playDestroy</code>, which restores what it touched. Curves hand the symbol a view-local quad instead of moving it.</li>
    <li><strong>Travel is not facing.</strong> Direction changes which way the strip moves and which edge feeds it; it never rotates or mirrors the art (ADR 017). <code>view.rotation</code> stays 0 in every combination.</li>
    <li><strong>EPS snap.</strong> <code>travel / pitch</code> is rounded to a whole number when within <code>1e-9</code>, so a travel of exactly N pitches lands on rotation N rather than N-1 when float residue leaves it a hair short. The remainder <code>off</code> is zeroed under the same epsilon.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The wrap cycle</h2>

  <Diagram height={560} caption="advance() derives rotation. Travel is one number; the whole-wrap count comes from it, the array is rotated until it matches, and _render() writes every position from index.">
    <Box x={180} y={20} w={360} h={60} title="advance(delta)" kind="primary" sub="delta = 0 returns at once" />
    <Box x={180} y={104} w={360} h={60} title="travel += polarity * delta" sub="odometer += |delta|" />
    <Box x={180} y={188} w={360} h={78} title="rot = floor(travel / pitch)" sub={['off = travel - rot * pitch', 'both snapped within 1e-9']} />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} to={[360, 104]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 164]} to={[360, 188]} />

    <Box x={40} y={326} w={300} h={78} title="_rotateToStart()" sub={['symbols.pop(), then unshift()', 'the wrapped symbol is index 0']} />
    <Box x={380} y={326} w={300} h={78} title="_rotateToEnd()" sub={['symbols.shift(), then push()', 'the wrapped symbol is index M-1']} />
    <Edge from={[360, 266]} via={[[360, 296], [190, 296]]} to={[190, 326]} label="rot rose" />
    <Edge from={[360, 266]} via={[[360, 296], [530, 296]]} to={[530, 326]} label="rot fell" />

    <Box x={180} y={464} w={360} h={60} title="_render()" kind="primary" sub="every view, plus the curve quad" />
    <Edge from={[190, 404]} via={[[190, 434], [360, 434]]} to={[360, 464]} />
    <Edge from={[530, 404]} via={[[530, 434], [360, 434]]} to={[360, 464]} />
    <Label x={360} y={550} text="main = (i - bufferStart) * pitch + off" size={14} mono tone="fg" anchor="middle" />
  </Diagram>

  <Diagram height={420} caption="The wrap callback picks an id. Each rotation hands the wrapped symbol to the Reel, which asks three sources in order; the first that answers wins, and the seat is refilled before _render() runs.">
    <Box x={200} y={20} w={320} h={60} title="Reel._onSymbolWrapped()" kind="accent" sub="called once per rotation" />
    <Box x={10} y={140} w={220} h={78} title="nudge queue" sub={['first: while a nudge', 'is in flight']} />
    <Box x={250} y={140} w={220} h={78} title="StopSequencer.next()" sub={['then: while isStopping', 'and hasRemaining']} />
    <Box x={490} y={140} w={220} h={78} title="RandomSymbolProvider" sub={["otherwise: next('spinning')", 'layered pools, injected rng']} />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} via={[[360, 110], [120, 110]]} to={[120, 140]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} to={[360, 140]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} via={[[360, 110], [600, 110]]} to={[600, 140]} />

    <Box x={40} y={278} w={300} h={78} title="_replaceSymbol(index, id)" sub={['release, acquire, resize,', 'place, then symbol:created']} />
    <Box x={380} y={278} w={300} h={60} title="refreshZIndex()" sub="every index shifted" />
    <Edge from={[120, 218]} via={[[120, 248], [190, 248]]} to={[190, 278]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 218]} via={[[360, 248], [190, 248]]} to={[190, 278]} />
    <Edge from={[600, 218]} via={[[600, 248], [190, 248]]} to={[190, 278]} />
    <Edge from={[340, 308]} to={[380, 308]} />
    <Label x={360} y={400} text="id in hand, back to _render()" anchor="middle" />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The Reel side of that loop lives in
    <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/Reel.ts</code>. <code>_onSymbolWrapped</code>
    chooses the id in the priority shown, then <code>_replaceSymbol</code>
    records the old view's reel-local main coordinate, and either installs an
    OCCUPIED stub (big-symbol tail), reuses the same instance on a same-id fast
    path (no pool round trip, no <code>symbol:created</code>), or releases the
    old symbol to the <code>SymbolFactory</code> and acquires the new one. The
    new view gets <code>resize(symbolWidth, symbolHeight)</code>, alpha 1,
    scale 1, its position, its z-index, a parent (the reel container, or the
    unmasked layer at rest) and then <code>symbol:created</code>. The pool
    mechanics are on the <a href="/architecture/symbols/">symbols page</a>; where
    the landing ids come from is the <a href="/architecture/frame-pipeline/">frame pipeline</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The axis</h2>

  <Diagram height={560} caption="ReelAxis, built once per reel by reelAxis(orientation, direction). The same ReelMotion code runs all four combinations; only the property key, the polarity and the feed edge differ. The arrow is travel; new ids enter at the feed edge.">
    {quads.map((q) => (
      <>
        <Group x={q.x} y={q.y} w={340} h={250} kind="solid" label={q.label} note={q.note} />
        {q.vertical ? (
          <>
            {[0, 1, 2].map((i) => <Cell x={q.x + 40} y={q.y + 52 + i * 48} size={44} label={['A', 'B', 'C'][i]} kind={i === 1 ? 'plain' : 'buffer'} />)}
            <Edge from={[q.x + 124, q.forward ? q.y + 56 : q.y + 188]} to={[q.x + 124, q.forward ? q.y + 188 : q.y + 56]} kind="primary" />
            <Label x={q.x + 144} y={q.y + 66} text={q.forward ? 'feed' : 'exit'} mono tone="faint" />
            <Label x={q.x + 144} y={q.y + 192} text={q.forward ? 'exit' : 'feed'} mono tone="faint" />
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            {[0, 1, 2].map((i) => <Cell x={q.x + 40 + i * 48} y={q.y + 56} size={44} label={['A', 'B', 'C'][i]} kind={i === 1 ? 'plain' : 'buffer'} />)}
            <Edge from={[q.forward ? q.x + 44 : q.x + 176, q.y + 130]} to={[q.forward ? q.x + 176 : q.x + 44, q.y + 130]} kind="primary" />
            <Label x={q.x + 40} y={q.y + 164} text={q.forward ? 'feed' : 'exit'} mono tone="faint" />
            <Label x={q.x + 180} y={q.y + 164} text={q.forward ? 'exit' : 'feed'} mono tone="faint" anchor="end" />
          </>
        )}
        <Label x={q.x + 16} y={q.y + 226} text={q.line} mono tone="fg" />
      </>
    ))}
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/ReelAxis.ts</code> is the only place that
    knows which screen property is "along the strip". It exposes
    <code>getMain / setMain / addMain</code>, <code>getCross / setCross</code>,
    and the two projections <code>toLocal(width, height)</code> to
    <code>{`{ cross, main }`}</code> and <code>toScreen(cross, main)</code> to
    <code>{`{ x, y }`}</code>. Public geometry stays screen-space
    (<code>symbolSize(w, h)</code>, <code>resize(w, h)</code>,
    <code>CellBounds</code>), which is why third-party symbol classes do not
    care about orientation. The builder constructs one axis per reel from
    <code>.orientation()</code> and <code>.direction()</code> /
    <code>.directionPerReel()</code>; there is no per-spin direction override.
    GSAP tweens in the phases are built from <code>axis.mainProp</code> as a
    computed key, which is what lets the bounce, the cascade fall and the pin
    squash work on any axis. Z-stacking is geometric, not travel-relative:
    <code>cellStacking</code> defaults to <code>'ascending'</code> (larger
    coordinate draws in front) and a reverse reel does not flip it.
    See the <a href="/guides/orientation-and-direction/">orientation guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>One tick</h2>

  <Diagram height={760} caption="The whole per-frame path, top to bottom: every reel in index order, then every active phase. Phases never write positions: they set reel.speed or reel.targetSpeed and the motion layer does the rest. A reel at speed 0 returns before computeDelta, so a parked reel never wraps.">
    <Box x={180} y={20} w={360} h={60} title="PIXI.Ticker" kind="code" sub="ticker.deltaMS" />
    <Box x={180} y={104} w={360} h={60} title="SpinController._onTick()" kind="accent" sub="returns at once unless _isSpinning" />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} to={[360, 104]} />

    <Group x={140} y={192} w={440} h={396} kind="tint" label="for each reel" />
    <Box x={180} y={232} w={360} h={60} title="Reel.update(deltaMs)" sub="dt = min(deltaMs, MAX_TICK_MS)" />
    <Box x={180} y={316} w={360} h={60} kind="ghost" title="stepDrive(state, target, dt)" sub="drive model only, then reel.speed" />
    <Box x={180} y={400} w={360} h={78} title="computeDelta(pitch, speed, dt)" sub={['StandardMode: pitch * speed * dt / 1000', 'clamped to half a pitch either way']} />
    <Box x={180} y={502} w={360} h={60} title="ReelMotion.advance(delta)" kind="primary" sub="rotations, wraps, then _render()" />
    <Edge from={[360, 164]} to={[360, 232]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 292]} to={[360, 316]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 376]} to={[360, 400]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 478]} to={[360, 502]} />

    <Group x={140} y={612} w={440} h={128} kind="tint" label="for each active phase" />
    <Box x={180} y={652} w={360} h={60} title="phase.update(deltaMs)" sub="StopPhase: tickSteps()" />
    <Edge from={[360, 588]} to={[360, 652]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    <code>SpinController</code> registers exactly one ticker callback
    (<code>packages/pixi-reels/src/spin/SpinController.ts</code>). The clamp to
    <code>MAX_TICK_MS</code> bounds the displacement when a backgrounded tab
    refocuses or a custom ticker reports a huge delta. <code>StandardMode</code>
    (<code>spin/modes/StandardMode.ts</code>) is the default
    <code>SpinningMode</code>; the sign of its result carries the
    <code>StartPhase</code> step-back pull (a brief negative speed) through
    unchanged, so "travel" means relative to the reel's own direction, not
    always positive. The motion layer also keeps an unsigned
    <code>odometer</code> that never resets; <code>Reel.travelledCells</code>
    divides it by the pitch, and <code>AnticipationPhase</code> uses it to end
    a tease after N cells instead of N milliseconds. <code>speedNormalized</code>
    is <code>speed / referenceSpeed</code>, where <code>referenceSpeed</code> is
    set to the active profile's <code>spinSpeed</code> at the start of every
    spin.
  </p>

  <h2>ReelDrive: bounded acceleration</h2>

  <p>
    By default a phase <em>tweens</em> <code>reel.speed</code>. With
    <code>builder.motionModel('drive', config)</code> the reel instead
    integrates toward <code>reel.targetSpeed</code> under acceleration bounds
    (<code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/ReelDrive.ts</code>). The config comes
    in two mutually exclusive forms and mixing them throws:
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Form</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Keys</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Resolved bounds</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
          <td class="px-4 py-2.5">profile-relative (recommended)</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs">accelFrames, decelFrames?, jerkFrames?</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80"><code>accel = spinSpeed / accelFrames</code>, <code>decel = spinSpeed / (decelFrames ?? accelFrames)</code>, <code>jerk = spinSpeed / jerkFrames</code> or 0. Re-resolved whenever <code>referenceSpeed</code> changes, so turbo accelerates harder than normal.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
          <td class="px-4 py-2.5">absolute</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs">accel, decel?, jerk?</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">Used as given, in speed units per frame squared (and cubed for jerk). Only right for a single-profile game: one bound makes super turbo take 53 frames to reach speed where normal takes 20.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>stepDrive(state, target, bounds, deltaMs)</code> runs once per reel
    per tick and mutates its state object to avoid per-frame garbage. In order:
    <code>dt = deltaMs / DRIVE_FRAME_MS</code> (a frame is <code>1000/60</code>
    ms); if the gap to the target is under <code>1e-4</code> it snaps to the
    target and zeroes acceleration; otherwise it picks <code>accel</code> when
    <code>|target| &gt; |speed|</code> and <code>decel</code> when slowing (a
    magnitude comparison, so the negative step-back pull lands on the right
    bound); with jerk it walks the acceleration toward that bound by at most
    <code>jerk * dt</code> and starts easing off once the remaining gap is below
    <code>accel^2 / (2 * jerk)</code>, the S-curve; then it steps the speed and
    clamps on crossing the target. <code>haltDrive()</code> zeroes speed,
    target and acceleration together, because <code>speed = 0</code> alone would
    ramp straight back toward the old target next tick; <code>forceSpeed(v)</code>
    jumps with no ramp and is what slams use. Under the drive model
    <code>StopPhase</code> sets <code>targetSpeed</code> for the spin-out instead
    of forcing it, and <code>land()</code> calls <code>haltDrive()</code>.
    The builder validates the config's shape at <code>build()</code> so a bad
    number names the <code>motionModel(...)</code> call.
  </p>

  <h2>The landing</h2>

  <Diagram height={160} caption="The animated stop is four steps inside StopPhase: wait out the stagger, load the frame and spin out through M wraps, land, bounce.">
    <Box x={8} y={40} w={128} h={78} title="delay" sub={['stop stagger', 'cut by quicken']} />
    <Box x={152} y={40} w={128} h={78} title="spinOut" sub={['setStopFrame()', 'isStopping']} />
    <Box x={296} y={40} w={128} h={78} title="M wraps" sub={['one id per wrap', 'until exhausted']} />
    <Box x={440} y={40} w={128} h={78} title="land()" kind="accent" sub={['snap, notify,', "'landing'"]} />
    <Box x={584} y={40} w={128} h={78} title="bounce()" kind="primary" sub={['overshoot,', 'then back']} />
    <Edge from={[136, 79]} to={[152, 79]} />
    <Edge from={[280, 79]} to={[296, 79]} />
    <Edge from={[424, 79]} to={[440, 79]} />
    <Edge from={[568, 79]} to={[584, 79]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    <code>land()</code> (<code>ReelPhase.land</code>) runs in a fixed order:
    <code>haltDrive()</code> zeroes speed, target speed and the drive state;
    <code>isStopping</code> goes false; <code>snapToGrid()</code> sets
    <code>travel = rot = off = 0</code>, re-renders, then
    <code>_finalizeFrame()</code> and <code>refreshZIndex()</code>;
    <code>notifySpinEnd()</code> calls <code>onReelSpinEnd()</code> on every
    strip symbol; and <code>notifyLanded(cells)</code> calls
    <code>onReelLanded(ctx)</code>, raises the reel event <code>'landing'</code>
    and the set event <code>spin:reelLanding</code>. <code>bounce()</code>
    (<code>ReelPhase.bounce</code>, <code>defaultBounce</code>) is two legs of
    half the duration each: <code>container[mainProp]</code> to
    <code>base + polarity * distance</code>, then back to base, with
    <code>bounceEase ?? 'power1.out'</code>; <code>followLifted</code> shifts
    unmasked views every frame; a distance of 0 or less returns an
    already-settled bounce. <code>spin:reelLanded</code> fires after it, via
    <code>_markLanded</code>.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={450} caption="The slam path: skip(), slamStop(), an abort, a timeout or a phase chain that threw. The full target frame is placed in one tick, buffers included, and nothing bounces.">
    <Group x={140} y={20} w={440} h={410} kind="tint" label="slam path" note="no steps, no bounce" />
    <Box x={180} y={60} w={360} h={78} title="forceComplete(ctx)" sub={['cancels the running step', 'ignores skippable']} />
    <Box x={180} y={162} w={360} h={78} title="StopPhase.onSkip" sub={['haltDrive(), isStopping = false', 'placeStrip(targetFrame)']} />
    <Box x={180} y={264} w={360} h={78} title="rest, then snap" sub={['container back to its base main', 'then reel.snapToGrid()']} />
    <Box x={180} y={366} w={360} h={44} title="controller lands it" kind="accent" />
    <Edge from={[360, 138]} to={[360, 162]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 240]} to={[360, 264]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 342]} to={[360, 366]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The full strip is placed, buffers included, so a big-symbol tail parked in
    <code>bufferStart</code> lands too. The container is rested before the snap
    so a mid-bounce slam cannot bake the overshoot into a lifted view. The
    controller then does what <code>land()</code> would have:
    <code>placeSymbols</code>, <code>notifySpinEnd</code>,
    <code>notifyLanded</code>, <code>_markLanded</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>Reel.setStopFrame(frame)</code> loads the strip into the
    <code>StopSequencer</code> (<code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/StopSequencer.ts</code>)
    with <code>axis.feedEdge</code>. The frame is always start-to-end order. A
    forward reel feeds from the start edge, so new symbols are pushed toward the
    end and the frame is consumed <strong>end-first</strong> (cursor starts at
    <code>length - 1</code>, step -1). A reverse reel consumes it
    <strong>head-first</strong> (cursor 0, step +1). Getting this backwards
    lands a correct-looking frame reversed, which is why the sequencer has a
    test per combination. <code>next()</code> throws when the frame is
    exhausted; the only caller gates on <code>hasRemaining</code>. Because the
    sequencer holds <code>M</code> ids, exactly <code>M</code> wraps happen
    during the spin-out and then the whole strip, buffers included, matches
    the frame. The spin-out keeps full <code>spinSpeed</code> unless the stop
    follows a tease (<code>preserveSpeed</code>): then the current slow speed
    is kept, floored at <code>8%</code> of <code>spinSpeed</code> and capped at
    <code>spinSpeed</code>. The stop and skip paths in full are on
    <a href="/architecture/spin-lifecycle/">spin lifecycle</a> and
    <a href="/architecture/skipping/">skipping</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Big-symbol occupancy</h2>

  <p>
    A big symbol is one id at its anchor cell plus OCCUPIED stubs in every
    other cell of its block. Stubs are invisible <code>ReelSymbol</code>s
    allocated by the reel, never pooled through the factory. The motion layer
    treats them like any other symbol: they wrap, they get a position, they
    carry the block's shape through the strip. On every snap
    <code>_finalizeFrame()</code> rebuilds <code>_occupancy</code>: it scans
    visible anchors first, then <code>bufferStart</code> anchors whose block
    reaches into the window, and resizes each anchor to
    <code>_blockSize(reels, cells)</code>, which includes the gaps (a 2×2 on
    80px cells with a 4px gap is 164px, not 160). <code>size.reels</code>
    always spans the cross axis and <code>size.cells</code> the main axis, so
    the screen width and height it maps to swap under horizontal.
    <code>getSymbolAt</code> and <code>getVisibleSymbols</code> resolve covered
    cells to the anchor's id. See <a href="/guides/big-symbols/">big symbols</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Nudge: a post-stop reposition</h2>

  <p>
    <code>Reel.nudge({`{ distance, direction, incoming, duration?, ease?, startDelay?, signal? }`})</code>
    shifts a reel at rest by whole cells and reveals caller-supplied ids. It
    throws if the reel is moving, stopping or already nudging; if
    <code>distance</code> is not a positive integer strictly below
    <code>M</code> (at <code>M</code> the strip would rotate fully and
    pre-placed buffer entries would be silently dropped); if
    <code>incoming.length !== distance</code>; if any incoming id is
    unregistered or is a big symbol; if a same-reel block would be split by
    the wrap boundary; or if a visible cell belongs to a cross-reel block.
    <code>direction</code> is relative to the reel's own axis, so
    <code>wrapsIntoStart = travelSign * polarity &gt; 0</code> decides which
    buffer the incoming ids are pre-placed into (up to that buffer's size) and
    the rest go into <code>_nudgeQueue</code>, padded with
    <code>'bufferStart'</code> / <code>'bufferEnd'</code> pool draws for wraps
    that only reach the off-screen buffer. The tween drives a 0..1 progress and
    feeds <code>motion.advance()</code> in steps of at most
    <code>0.45 * pitch</code>, clamped to the intended total so an overshooting
    ease cannot fire a spurious wrap. Default <code>duration</code> is
    <code>200 * distance</code> ms, default ease <code>'power2.out'</code>. The
    per-wrap <code>refreshZIndex</code> is deferred to the final
    <code>snapToGrid()</code>. Events on the set bus: <code>nudge:start</code>,
    <code>nudge:complete</code>, <code>nudge:cancelled</code>; the reel emits
    <code>phase:enter</code> / <code>phase:exit</code> with the name
    <code>'nudge'</code>. <code>skipNudge()</code> jumps to the landed state.
    See the <a href="/guides/nudge/">nudge guide</a> and the
    <a href="/recipes/nudge/">nudge recipes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Per-reel geometry</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>extent</code></strong> is the reel box's length along the strip, from <code>reelExtents([...])</code>, <code>multiways({`{ reelExtent }`})</code>, or <code>cells * cellMain + (cells - 1) * mainGap</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>mainOffset</code></strong> places a shorter reel inside the tallest strip by <code>reelAnchor</code>: <code>'start'</code> gives 0, <code>'end'</code> gives <code>tallest - extent</code>, <code>'center'</code> (the default) gives <code>(tallest - extent) / 2</code>. It is written to <code>container[mainProp]</code> and re-baked into any lifted unmask view by <code>_syncUnmaskedViewOffsets</code> after every motion write.</li>
    <li><strong><code>cellMain</code></strong> is the per-reel cell size along the strip: <code>(extent - (cells - 1) * mainGap) / cells</code> when explicit extents are set, otherwise the symbol size itself. <code>spinCellSize</code> is the cell every MultiWays reel spins on; <code>AdjustPhase</code> reshapes to the landed size afterwards through <code>Reel.reshape</code> → <code>ReelMotion.reshape</code>, which only refreshes pitch and <code>bufferStart</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>symbolWidth</code> / <code>symbolHeight</code></strong> are <code>axis.toScreen(cellCross, cellMain)</code>, the pair every <code>resize()</code> receives.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="/guides/per-reel-geometry/">per-reel geometry guide</a> shows
    pyramids and jagged boards built from these.
  </p>

  <h2>Caveats, from reading the code</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Speed units.</strong> The <code>ReelDrive</code> comments call <code>reel.speed</code> "pixels per frame", but <code>StandardMode</code> computes <code>slotPitch * speed * deltaMs / 1000</code>, so <code>spinSpeed: 30</code> moves 30 cell pitches per second (half a pitch per 60 fps frame) regardless of cell size. The drive bounds are in the same unit per frame. The code is the source of truth here.</li>
    <li><strong>Vertical-only wording.</strong> <code>ReelMotion</code>'s constructor still names its parameters <code>symbolHeight</code> and <code>symbolGapY</code>, <code>Reel.update</code> calls its result <code>deltaY</code>, and the <code>StandardMode</code> and <code>StopSequencer</code> headers describe a strip "scrolling downward". The behaviour is axis-generic; the names are v1 leftovers.</li>
  </ul>

